<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleni of Ethiopia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1522">1522</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Empress Eleni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleni was an Ethiopian empress, born a princess of the Muslim Hadiya Sultanate, who married Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. She remained a powerful figure at court under several later emperors and acted as regent for the young Lebna Dengel. Fearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, she sent the envoy Mateus to Portugal around 1509 to seek an alliance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleni-of-ethiopia.jpg" alt="Eleni of Ethiopia" width="300" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, but died over 70 years ago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eleni_of_Hadiya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eleni, whose name is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a> form of Helena and who was also known as Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, was the daughter of the King of Hadiya. The Hadiyan leader offered her in marriage to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> as part of a peace accord. The Adalite writer Arab Faqih later wrote that the Muslims of Hadiya had been forced under threat of persecution to give their daughters to the Christian state, and the historian Richard Pankhurst saw the marriage as a measure designed to undermine the alliance between Adal and Hadiya. The Portuguese historian Baltasar Teles wrote that she had no children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>'s death his son by another wife, Baeda Maryam I, whose own mother Tsion Mogasa had been beaten to death, gave Eleni the title of Queen Mother, and the historian Paul B. Henze judged her practically co-monarch during his reign. Under Emperor Eskender she was at first pushed aside by the Bitwoded Amda Mikael, but around 1486 she took part in a palace coup that led to his deposition and execution, and she again played a leading part in government under Emperor Na'od. The manuscript Bruce 88 records that she lived in the palaces of three emperors. Aware of the wider Muslim world from childhood, she sought reconciliation and trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p><p>When Na'od died in battle, the Abuna Marqos told the missionary Francisco Álvares that he and Eleni had made Lebna Dengel king, because they had all the great men in their hands. She was chief regent for the young emperor alongside his mother, Na'od Mogassa, and Ras Degelhan of Gojjam, and with the advice of Pero da Covilhã she sent Mateus, known as Matthew the Armenian, to the King of Portugal and the Pope.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eleni held extensive estates in Gojjam and was praised for her piety, her knowledge of books and law and her grasp of affairs of state. Henze says she died at an advanced age in the 1520s, and the scholars Beckingham and Huntingford date her death from Álvares's account to April 1522. Álvares, who was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> at the time, recorded the grief at court, where people said that while she lived all were defended and protected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eleni of Ethiopia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relations were tense: Garad Mahiko rebelled against Zara Yaqob in the 15th century, while a Hadiya princess, <b>Eleni</b>, married the emperor and became a powerful empress dowager.</blockquote><a href="/state/hadiya-sultanate">Hadiya Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Hadiya Kingdom. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9,40&amp;year=1512" aria-label="Ethiopia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5655 1828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Eleni of Ethiopia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6105 2098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6105 2098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=9,40&amp;year=1512">What was here in 1512? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
